  "query_preview": "..\n...WINDY CONDITIONS FRIDAY...\n\nFor thunderstorms, ensembles bring the best chances late \nmorning to middle afternoon. Moderate Theta-e values across the \nregion support convection with the front passage. As the front \npasses, indices Cape values are in the 200-400 J/kg. Lapse rates\nare +6. The best chances are over the Cascades and northern \nmountains. These areas have 20-30 percent chance for \nthunderstorms. The region has less than 20 percent chance. Main \nimpacts will be lightning, gusty outflows, and brief heavy \ndownpours. \n\nCold, dry air will fill in behind the exiting front. Winds in \nthe 850-700mb range from 35-45 mph. Behind the cold front, low \nlevel winds to mix to the surface and bring gusty winds to most \nof the Basin into the early evening. Models have a more gradual \ndrop in the Theta-e values than previous runs. The change has \nled to a slightly lower winds speed forecast. There is 50 \npercent probability for winds 40+ mph across the Basin. An \npocket of 50+ mph is possible over the Waterville Plateau and \nCentral Basin. Blowing dust is possible but is dependent on the \namount of rain from earlier in the day. Possible impacts include\nairborne objects, difficult travel for high profile vehicles, \nand isolated power outages.\n\nSaturday through Tuesday: Behind the system, the region will be\nunder the influence of a cool, dry shallow trough pattern. Any \nprecip chances will be near zero by midday Saturday. It will \ndefinitely feel like autumn across the region. Highs will be in \nthe upper 50s to low 60s. Overnight lows will be in the mid 30s \nto 40s. Frost could be an issue for areas in the Cascades and \nnorthern mountains. /JDC",
